---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="What to Document - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="documentation" subChapterId="what-to-document">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Some types of docs have an outsized impact on both human productivity and AI output
				quality. In a{" "}
				<a href="/requirements-gathering/documentation-first-development" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">documentation-first</a>{" "}
				project these are the documents that become your source of truth, so they are worth
				writing well. You can prioritize with the following list.
			</p>

			<h3>High-Impact Documentation Types</h3>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						Requirements
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						Clear product requirements give the AI (and developers) the "what" and
						"why" behind a feature. When the AI can reference a requirements doc, it
						makes better implementation decisions because it understands the goal, not
						just the code. Strategy teams produce these; developers consume them.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						Task Breakdowns
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						Detailed task breakdowns with context about the current codebase help the
						AI scope its work appropriately. Instead of trying to solve an entire
						feature at once, it can focus on a well-defined piece.
						See <a href="/task-breakdowns/including-context">Including Context</a>{" "}
						for how to structure these.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						Tickets
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						Well-structured Jira or Linear tickets serve as focused instructions for
						the AI. A ticket with clear acceptance criteria, relevant file paths, and
						context about related work gives the AI everything it needs to start
						coding. Vague tickets produce vague code from both humans and AI.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						Runbooks
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						Step-by-step guides for operational tasks: how to deploy, how to roll back,
						how to diagnose common issues. These are useful for the AI when debugging
						production problems or when a developer asks "how do I deploy this?"
						The AI can walk them through the process if the runbook exists.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						Guides and Skills
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						Guides explain how things work. Skills tell the AI how to do specific tasks.
						Together they capture your team's best practices in a reusable way.
						See <a href="/requirements-writing/skills">Skills</a> for how to build
						these for your AI tools.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						Test Plans
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						Documents that describe what to test and how to test it for a feature.
						These help the AI generate meaningful tests rather than boilerplate
						assertions. They also help QA and strategy team members verify that the
						implementation covers the expected scenarios. Once a feature is complete,
						AI is great at taking a first pass at writing a test plan based on the
						code and requirements, giving your team a solid starting point to review
						and refine.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						Decision Logs (ADRs)
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						Architecture Decision Records capture the context, options considered,
						and reasoning behind technical choices. Without these, the AI (and new
						team members) will question or undo deliberate decisions. "We chose Redis
						over Memcached because..." prevents a lot of wasted conversations.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border-l-4 border-l-teal-400 pl-4 py-2">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800">
						External Specs and Third-Party Docs
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-1">
						The contracts you don't own but have to build against: a vendor's API, a
						partner's data format, another team's service. The AI can't open a supplier
						PDF, a Confluence page in another space, or a spec that sits behind a login,
						so it guesses at the shape and gets it wrong. Pull the relevant parts into
						a <code>docs/external/</code> folder so the AI builds against the real contract.
						This also versions the spec alongside your code, so when the source changes
						you can see what your integration assumed.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3>Keeping Documentation Current</h3>

			<p>
				The hardest part of documentation has always been maintenance. Here are patterns
				that work well with AI-assisted workflows:
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
						Update docs as part of every task
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						After the AI finishes implementing a feature, ask it to update the relevant
						documentation. The context is fresh and the cost is minimal. Make this part
						of your definition of done.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
						Periodic doc audits with AI
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						Every few sprints, ask the AI to compare your documentation against the
						actual code. "Read docs/auth.md and the code in src/auth/. Are there any
						discrepancies?" This catches drift before it becomes a problem.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-sm font-semibold text-stone-800 mb-1">
						Documentation in code reviews
					</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						When reviewing PRs, check whether relevant docs were updated. This is a
						quick check that prevents documentation from going stale. If they weren't
						updated, ask the AI to draft the updates right in the review.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					You don't need all of these document types on day one. Start with what's
					causing the most pain. If the AI keeps making bad architectural choices,
					write decision logs. If tickets are vague and producing bad code, improve
					your ticket templates. Work outward from the problems you're actually having.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
